From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: shortcut keys for X11 apps in the background

Under Xwayland an X11 app sees keys only while one of its windows has the
focus, so X11 apps that watch the keyboard themselves (the official
Discord client's push-to-talk) stop working when another window is in
front. KDE forwards keys to Xwayland for this; GNOME does not. NickelOS
forwards what cannot be text:

- NickelX11Keys, a pre-target handler after ChromeOS's accelerators,
  passes on the modifier keys (not AltGr), keys pressed with Ctrl, Alt or
  Search, and the function keys, never on the lock screen. Releases are
  watched before the accelerators (tapping Search opens the launcher on
  its release, which the accelerator takes), a second press that is not a
  repeat releases first, and a held modifier the next key event shows up
  is released: no key stays held in Xwayland.
- They go to nickel-x11-keys in the Linux session, which connects with
  VmApplicationsService.NickelX11KeysConnect(h socket) and presses them in
  Xwayland (XTest) while no X11 window has the focus.
- Settings > Apps > Linux apps: "Shortcut keys for older Linux apps", on
  by default (guest_os pref nickel.x11_key_forwarding).

The strings are in ui_chromeos_strings.grd.

